// std/core/str.lls — FACADE for the owning UTF-8 string type `Str`.
//
// L-022 phase 3 (2026-07-14): str.lls used to be one ~930-line module because
// the compiler could not link inherent `methods Str` blocks that live in a
// different module than `struct Str`. With L-022 fixed (cross-module inherent
// method mangling + facade propagation + imported-type impl-key recovery), the
// string API is split by domain across an ACYCLIC three-layer DAG:
//
//   std.core.str_core   : struct Str + StrSlice + lifecycle hooks + the
//                         foundational methods (capacity / queries / byte build /
//                         construction / equality / comparison / substr / copy /
//                         zero-copy slice views) + trait impls (Clone / Destroy /
//                         Equal / Hash / Add / Order) + all StrSlice methods.
//   std.core.str_search : search + transform + replace/pad + collection methods
//                         (find / rfind / count / contains? / starts_with? /
//                         ends_with? / upper / lower / trim / concat / repeat /
//                         replace / pad_left / pad_right / bytes / split / lines).
//   std.core.str_num    : numeric parsing (to_int / to_i64 / to_float / to_bool).
//
// This file re-exports all three so `import std.core.str` keeps working with
// ZERO consumer changes. The prelude injects `import std.core.str` into every
// root program (ast.c:151), and Phase 2's propagate_inherited_methods carries
// the submodules' inherent `methods Str` through this facade to the consumer's
// impl registry — so `"x".find(..)`, `.to_int()`, `.upper()` etc. all resolve
// exactly as they did when everything lived in one file.
//
// Str's owning module is std.core.str_core, so its mangled type prefix is
// std_core_str_core__Str. Call sites follow the struct's llvm_name (B-2), so the
// migration is transparent across JIT and AOT.

import std.core.str_core
import std.core.str_search
import std.core.str_num
